  "description": "Faithful replication with the original photographs: twenty close-up window views of the facade of a nearby building in residential neighbourhoods of Ljubljana, Slovenia, each covering two floors of the opposite building at about the same distance, photographed on one cloudy day in May 2021 from different architectural periods, with different elements and upkeep; the views deliberately leave out elements usually seen as good for visual comfort, and on five of the facades the authors added vegetation in Adobe Photoshop. Participants imagine that this is the view from their window and, for each of the twenty views, choose the paper's reaction to the view (11 options: frightening, scary, dangerous; incomprehensible, unpleasant; very disturbing, repellent, depressing; boring; soothing, pleasant; understandable, coherent, legible; attractive, fascinating, invigorating; dreamy, romantic; mysterious; acceptable, unobtrusive; does not evoke a determined response, neutral) and the reason that triggered it (7 options: surface characteristic, composition quality, narrative quality, complexity, dynamics, unpleasant/pleasant space, other). Gender, age and the type of settlement they live in are asked first. Rebuilt from Drobne, Zbašnik-Senegačnik, Kristl, Koprivec & Fikfak (2022), Analysis of the Window Views of the Nearby Façades, Sustainability 14(1): 269, with the photographs cropped from its Figure 2 and the reactions and reasons from its Table 1 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: W01-W20 = window views 1-20; answers are in reaction (x1-x11) and reason (y1-y7), keyed by view. Analysis as in the paper: per view, the share of negative reactions (x1-x4), the most, second and third most frequent reaction and reason, the coefficient of variation of the reactions and F-tests of the homogeneity of the reaction and reason groups; the paper (98 third-year architecture students in Ljubljana) found negative reactions predominant for 12 of the 20 views, boring the most frequent reaction for many plain facades, more agreement on pleasant, attractive and acceptable views than on unpleasant ones, views with an interesting composition or greenery rated quite positively, and surface characteristics the main reason for negative reactions. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper's 52-question Moodle survey for one class of architecture students also asked about the institution where respondents live, their green spaces and contact with nature, whose wording is not printed, so only gender, age and settlement type are asked, in our wording; here anyone can take part; (2) the question wording, the instruction text and the view order (1 to 20, as numbered in the paper) are ours; (3) the photographs are the figure copies (about 400 x 270 px; the originals were resized to 480 x 320 px).",
